// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Logical model — retail point-of-sale, 3NF.
//
// What belongs here: entities, their attributes, keys, and relationships, in
// third normal form. This is the logical phase: it comes after docs/conceptual.md
// is agreed and before any migration is written. The DDL in migrations/ is
// derived from this file, never the reverse.
//
// Done when: every entity in docs/conceptual.md appears here with attributes and
// a key; every relationship has an explicit Ref with cardinality; every table
// and column has a Note; 3NF holds with no transitive dependency anyone has to
// defend; and every business term used matches docs/glossary.md exactly.
//
// Must NOT go here: Postgres-specific types, indexes, partitions, tablespaces,
// storage parameters, or anything about performance — those are physical, they
// live in the migration. No facts, no dimensions, no star schemas, ever: this is
// the source system.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Conventions
//
// Naming
//   tables            singular, snake_case, business language: transaction_line
//                     not TransactionLines, not txn_ln
//   columns           snake_case; no table-name prefix (transaction.id, not
//                     transaction.transaction_id)
//   primary key       id
//   foreign key       <referenced_table>_id
//   booleans          is_ / has_ prefix
//   timestamps        _at suffix (system time); dates _date suffix (business time)
//   money             amount / _amount suffix, with the currency stated in a Note
//   no abbreviations  qty, txn, cust, amt are all rejected in review
//   reserved words    never used as identifiers, not even quoted
//
// Types (logical, not Postgres)
//   text, integer, decimal(p,s), boolean, date, timestamp, uuid
//   Map to Postgres in the migration, not here.
//
// Notes
//   Every table and every column carries a Note. The Note text is what becomes
//   the COMMENT ON in the migration — write it once, here, and carry it across.
//   A column whose Note only restates its name is not documented.
//
// Relationships
//   One Ref per relationship, cardinality explicit (>, <, -, <>). Resolve
//   many-to-many with a named associative entity that has its own business
//   meaning — if you cannot name it in business language, the relationship is
//   wrong.
//
// Enums
//   Use an Enum only for a closed set the business controls and rarely changes.
//   Anything a store manager can add to is a lookup table, not an Enum.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Project retail_oltp {
  database_type: 'PostgreSQL'
  Note: 'Source system for retail point-of-sale. 3NF OLTP. Not a warehouse.'
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Phase-2 decisions made while translating docs/conceptual.md
//
// Four places below make a call the conceptual model left to this phase. Each
// is recorded as a real decision, not just a note — an ADR where the choice is
// hard to reverse, an in-place update to conceptual.md where it isn't.
//
// 1. sale_transaction.sequence_number and the composite natural keys on
//    store_assortment, transaction_line and tender. conceptual.md defines no
//    transaction identifier at all (register is out of scope under A2), so a
//    per-store, per-business-date sequence is introduced here as the natural
//    key. Revisit if the business already has a different receipt-numbering
//    scheme. Not ADR-worthy on its own: reversible in one migration.
//
// 2. Type lives on transaction_line, not sale_transaction (ADR 0004).
//    conceptual.md's Modelling decisions section said the transaction "carries
//    a transaction type", but separately flagged an even exchange — a return
//    line and a sale line settled in one visit — as "for phase 2 to confirm
//    rather than assume". A header-level type cannot represent that, so the
//    type moved to the line. conceptual.md is updated in place to match.
//
// 3. transaction_line.store_id is a deliberate denormalization (ADR 0003),
//    duplicating data reachable from sale_transaction_id. Accepted because it
//    is what lets invariant I3 be enforced by a composite foreign key instead
//    of trusted application code. See docs/physical-design.md for the
//    consistency argument.
//
// 4. customer.phone_number (decision D3 in docs/conceptual.md). conceptual.md
//    left CUSTOMER with no identifying attribute at all — unusable as an
//    entity. Phone number is the default: the minimum the business already
//    collects for a receipt lookup or a no-receipt return, chosen because no
//    loyalty program exists to key off instead (assumption A3).
//
// 5. A second attribute pass added two invariants to conceptual.md that
//    cardinality cannot express, the same way I1-I4 could not be: I5 (a
//    line's tax agrees with its product's taxability) and I6 (a line only
//    sells what its store was actively carrying on that date, once
//    store_assortment gained carried_from/carried_to). Also added a `state`
//    lookup table for store.state_code — the same closed-list-to-lookup
//    pattern already used for tender_type and movement_reason, not a new
//    exception.
//
// 6. A trimming pass removed four things that didn't earn their place:
//    product.is_weighted (fully derivable from unit_of_measure_code, given
//    this model's actual unit values), product.is_active (nothing reads it;
//    saleability is already governed per store by store_assortment.carried_to),
//    and store.street_address/city/postal_code (no invariant or process in
//    this model uses them — state_code is kept, since I5 and a future
//    jurisdiction entity actually need it). Also dropped transaction_line's
//    surrogate id, since (sale_transaction_id, line_number) already
//    identifies a line uniquely; reverses_line_id and
//    inventory_movement.transaction_line_id became composite references to
//    that natural key instead of pointing at a surrogate that added nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lookups — closed lists the business controls, excluded from entity status
// in docs/conceptual.md ("A closed list of codes with a name and nothing else
// is a domain, not an entity"). Modelled as tables with a natural `code` key,
// not as Enums, because seeds/README.md commits them as editable seed data
// (seeds/tender_type.csv, seeds/reason_code.csv) — the DBML convention reserves
// Enum for a set a store manager cannot add to.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Table unit_of_measure {
  code text [pk, note: 'Natural key. EACH for unit items; a weight unit (LB, KG, OZ) for items sold by weight.']
  name text [not null, note: 'Business-facing name of the unit.']

  Note: 'The unit a product is sold in. Named as excluded from entity status in docs/conceptual.md; kept as a lookup because transaction_line.quantity is meaningless without it.'
}

Table tender_type {
  code text [pk, note: 'Natural key, e.g. CASH, CARD, GIFT_CARD, EBT, CHEQUE.']
  name text [not null, note: 'Business-facing name of the tender type.']

  Note: 'Closed list of settlement methods (docs/glossary.md: tender). A lookup, not an entity, per docs/conceptual.md. Committed as seed data: seeds/tender_type.csv.'
}

Table movement_reason {
  code text [pk, note: 'Natural key, e.g. SALE, RETURN, RECEIPT, TRANSFER, SHRINK, ADJUSTMENT, SALVAGE.']
  name text [not null, note: 'Business-facing name of the reason.']

  Note: 'Closed list of reasons a stock movement happened (docs/glossary.md: inventory movement). A lookup, not an entity, per docs/conceptual.md. Committed as seed data: seeds/reason_code.csv.'
}

Table condition_code {
  code text [pk, note: 'Natural key, e.g. RESALABLE, DAMAGED, DESTROYED, CLAIMS_HOLD.']
  name text [not null, note: 'Business-facing name of the condition.']

  Note: '''Closed list of conditions a returned item can be found in. Condition decides where a return's goods go - back to shelf, to salvage, to a claims hold, or nowhere if destroyed at the counter (docs/conceptual.md, Modelling decisions). A lookup, not an entity.'''
}

Table state {
  code text [pk, note: 'Natural key. Two-letter USPS code, e.g. CA, TX, NY.']
  name text [not null, note: 'Full state name.']

  Note: '''Closed list of US states and territories a store can sit in. A lookup, not an entity, for the same reason as tender_type and movement_reason - a code with a name and nothing else. Chosen deliberately, not just for consistency: A5 in docs/conceptual.md names tax-by-jurisdiction as the assumption most likely to fail, and a normalized state_code is what lets that be added later without redoing store.'''
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Core entities — the eight from docs/conceptual.md.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Table store {
  id             integer [pk, increment, note: 'Surrogate key. Never exposed to the business.']
  code           text    [not null, unique, note: 'Operator-facing store number. Stable for the life of the store. Natural key.']
  name           text    [not null, note: 'Store name as used day to day. Not a legal entity name (docs/glossary.md).']
  state_code     text    [not null, ref: > state.code, note: '''The state or territory the store sits in. The only address component kept: it feeds invariant I5's tax computation and is where a future tax-jurisdiction entity would attach (A5). Street address, city and postal code were considered and deliberately left out - none of them feed any invariant or process this model runs; they would be a real store record's business, not this OLTP system's.''']
  timezone       text    [not null, note: '''IANA timezone name (e.g. America/Chicago). Drives sale_transaction.business_date: decision D2 dates a sale to the calendar date at this store's local midnight, not the system clock's.''']
  sales_tax_rate decimal(5,4) [not null, note: '''Single tax rate charged by this store, applied at sale time to a taxable line (product.is_taxable). One rate per store, not per jurisdiction or per product category - A5 in docs/conceptual.md names tax-by-jurisdiction as the assumption most likely to fail, and this is the column that would need to move to a jurisdiction entity if it does. Combines with product.is_taxable to produce transaction_line.tax_amount; see invariant I5.''']
  opened_date    date    [not null, note: 'Date the store began trading. Together with closed_date, this is what tests/data_quality/ needs to assert "no store rings sales before it opened".']
  closed_date    date    [note: 'Date the store stopped trading, if it has. Null while the store is still open.']

  Note: 'A location that holds stock or processes a sale. Not a legal entity and not a reporting rollup (docs/glossary.md).'
}

Table product {
  id                   integer [pk, increment, note: 'Surrogate key.']
  sku                  text    [not null, unique, note: 'Chain-wide stock keeping unit. Defined once for the whole chain; natural key.']
  upc                  text    [unique, note: 'Barcode. Nullable and separate from sku: some own-brand items carry no UPC at all.']
  name                 text    [not null, note: 'Product description as shown on shelf tag and receipt.']
  unit_of_measure_code text    [not null, ref: > unit_of_measure.code, note: '''The unit this product is sold in. Determines whether transaction_line.quantity is a whole number or a weighed fraction - EACH means counted, anything else (LB, KG, OZ) means weighed at the register, so a separate is_weighted flag would only restate this column.''']
  is_taxable           boolean [not null, note: '''Whether this product is subject to sales tax at all - groceries are typically exempt, prepared food is not (docs/conceptual.md, A5). Combined with store.sales_tax_rate at sale time to produce transaction_line.tax_amount; see invariant I5.''']

  Note: '''An item the retailer sells, defined once for the whole chain. Carries no price and no store - both vary by store and by state (docs/conceptual.md). Also carries no chain-wide is_active: nothing in this model reads one, and a product's actual saleability is already governed per store by store_assortment.carried_to - a second, independently-maintained flag here would just be an unenforced copy of that fact.'''
}

Table store_assortment {
  store_id     integer       [not null, ref: > store.id, note: 'The store carrying this product.']
  product_id   integer       [not null, ref: > product.id, note: 'The product carried at this store.']
  price_amount decimal(10,2) [not null, note: '''Current price this store sells the product at, in USD. Prices vary by store and by state, which is why price lives here and not on product (docs/conceptual.md). Current-only: this column holds no price history.''']
  carried_from date          [not null, note: 'Date this store started carrying this product.']
  carried_to   date          [note: '''Date this store stopped carrying this product. Null while still carried. Answers the open question in docs/glossary.md's assortment entry - a de-assorted product does not leave the table, it gets a carried_to; remaining shelf stock is a separate fact tracked by inventory_movement, not by this row. See invariant I6.''']

  indexes {
    (store_id, product_id) [pk]
  }

  Note: '''A product as offered at one store - carried here, at this store's price, for this period. Associative entity; the business's own noun for it (docs/conceptual.md). Composite primary key (store_id, product_id): this row represents one carry period per store-product pair, not one row per period. A real limitation, stated rather than hidden - a product de-assorted and later re-assorted at the same store has no way to keep both periods, since updating carried_from forward would falsely invalidate invariant I6 against transaction_line rows sold during the earlier period. Revisit if re-assortment after a gap turns out to matter: it would need the key to include a period, which ripples into every foreign key that currently points at (store_id, product_id).'''
}

Table customer {
  id           integer [pk, increment, note: 'Surrogate key. Never exposed to the business.']
  phone_number text    [not null, unique, note: '''Natural key. The phone number a cashier keys in for a receipt lookup or a no-receipt return - the minimum identifying detail the business already collects, chosen because no loyalty program exists to key off instead (assumption A3; decision D3 in docs/conceptual.md).''']

  Note: 'The party identified on a purchase. Optional throughout - most in-store baskets are anonymous (docs/glossary.md).'
}

Table sale_transaction {
  id               integer          [pk, increment, note: 'Surrogate key.']
  store_id         integer          [not null, ref: > store.id, note: 'Where the checkout happened. Attribution is store-level only - no register, no employee (assumption A2).']
  customer_id      integer          [ref: > customer.id, note: 'The identified party on this purchase, if any. Nullable: anonymous baskets are the norm in store.']
  business_date    date             [not null, note: '''The store's local calendar date at settlement (decision D2). Distinct from occurred_at, which is the system timestamp.''']
  sequence_number  integer          [not null, note: 'Per-store, per-business-date sequence. A phase-2 addition: conceptual.md defines no transaction identifier because register is out of scope (A2). See the phase-2 decisions note at the top of this file.']
  occurred_at      timestamp        [not null, note: 'System timestamp when the transaction settled (tender accepted). Distinct from business_date.']

  indexes {
    (store_id, business_date, sequence_number) [unique, note: 'Natural key: a transaction is identified within its store and business day by this sequence.']
    (id, store_id) [unique, note: '''Supports the composite foreign key from transaction_line that declaratively enforces invariant I3 - a line's assortment belongs to the transaction's store.''']
  }

  Note: '''A completed checkout: a sale, a return, or an exchange of both in one visit. The only financial header in the model (docs/conceptual.md). Carries no type of its own - each line carries whether it sold or reversed something (ADR 0004) - and no reference to an original transaction, since a return's originals are derived from its lines' reverses_sale_transaction_id/reverses_line_number (ADR 0002).'''
}

Enum line_type {
  sale
  return
}

Table transaction_line {
  sale_transaction_id       integer       [not null, note: '''The transaction this line belongs to. Half of this table's natural primary key.''']
  line_number               integer       [not null, note: '''Position of this line within its transaction. Half of this table's natural primary key, together with sale_transaction_id - no surrogate id, since this pair already identifies a line uniquely and every reference to a line (below, and from inventory_movement) can just use it directly.''']
  store_id                  integer       [not null, note: '''Copy of the transaction's store. Carried here rather than looked up, so a composite foreign key can pin this line's assortment to the same store as its transaction - the declarative enforcement of invariant I3, accepted as a denormalization in ADR 0003.''']
  product_id                integer       [not null, note: 'The product sold or returned on this line.']
  line_type                 line_type     [not null, note: '''Whether this line sold or reversed something. Lives on the line, not the transaction header, so one transaction can hold both types at once - an even exchange - which a header-level type could not represent (ADR 0004, resolving the edge case docs/conceptual.md flagged as open under invariant I4).''']
  quantity                  decimal(10,3) [not null, note: '''Quantity sold or returned, in the product's unit of measure. Weighed items (produce, meat, deli) sell in fractional amounts.''']
  unit_price_amount         decimal(10,2) [not null, note: '''Price actually charged for this line, in USD, captured at sale time. Never recomputed from the current assortment price, so a refund reverses what was really paid however far the shelf price has since moved (docs/conceptual.md).''']
  tax_amount                decimal(10,2) [not null, note: '''Tax charged on this line, in USD - one amount per line, no per-jurisdiction breakdown (assumption A5). Captured at sale time from product.is_taxable and store.sales_tax_rate, same as unit_price_amount: never recomputed, so a rate change or a taxability reclassification never rewrites a completed sale. Zero when the product is not taxable; see invariant I5.''']
  reverses_sale_transaction_id integer    [note: 'Which transaction the original line this line reverses belongs to, if line_type is return. Half of a composite self-reference (with reverses_line_number), since this table has no surrogate id to point at instead. Nullable: no-receipt returns carry no reference at all (ADR 0002).']
  reverses_line_number      integer       [note: 'Which line, within that transaction, this line reverses. The other half of the composite self-reference. Nullable together with reverses_sale_transaction_id.']
  condition_code            text          [ref: > condition_code.code, note: 'Condition the returned item was found in. Set only when line_type is return; it decides which inventory_movement(s), if any, this line produces (docs/conceptual.md, Modelling decisions).']

  indexes {
    (sale_transaction_id, line_number) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One item within a transaction. The atom of the business (docs/conceptual.md).'
}

Table tender {
  sale_transaction_id integer       [not null, ref: > sale_transaction.id, note: 'The transaction this tender settles.']
  tender_sequence     integer       [not null, note: 'Position of this tender within its transaction. Natural key together with sale_transaction_id - split tender means more than one row per transaction.']
  tender_type_code    text          [not null, ref: > tender_type.code, note: 'The means of settlement - cash, card, gift card, EBT, cheque.']
  amount              decimal(10,2) [not null, note: 'Amount settled by this tender, in USD. Negative on a refund (docs/glossary.md).']
  auth_code           text          [note: '''Card authorization code. Card tenders only, null otherwise - phase 3 should add a CHECK tying this to tender_type_code rather than trusting it stays consistent. PCI surface: retained data, not full card data.''']
  card_last_four      text          [note: 'Last four digits of the card number. Card tenders only, null otherwise - same conditional CHECK as auth_code. PCI surface.']

  indexes {
    (sale_transaction_id, tender_sequence) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One means of settlement applied to a transaction. A basket settled by gift card and debit together is two rows, which is why this is an entity and not an amount on the header (docs/glossary.md).'
}

Table inventory_movement {
  id                   integer       [pk, increment, note: 'Surrogate key. No natural key beyond it: this is an append-only event ledger, not a thing with a business identity of its own.']
  store_id             integer       [not null, note: 'The store where stock changed.']
  product_id           integer       [not null, note: 'The product whose stock changed.']
  quantity_delta       decimal(10,3) [not null, note: 'Signed change in on-hand quantity. Negative for a sale or shrink, positive for a receipt or a return to shelf. On-hand is the running sum of this column, never a stored position (assumption A4).']
  movement_reason_code text          [not null, ref: > movement_reason.code, note: 'What caused this movement - sale, return, receipt, transfer, shrink, adjustment, salvage.']
  line_sale_transaction_id integer   [note: '''Which transaction the line that triggered this movement belongs to, if any. Half of a composite reference (with line_number) into transaction_line, which has no surrogate id to point at instead. Nullable: most movements - receipts, transfers, shrink, cycle counts, salvage - have no line behind them (docs/conceptual.md, Modelling decisions).''']
  line_number          integer       [note: 'Which line, within that transaction, triggered this movement. The other half of the composite reference. Nullable together with line_sale_transaction_id.']
  occurred_at          timestamp     [not null, note: 'When this movement was recorded. Immutable: a correction is another movement, never an edit to this one.']

  Note: 'A single stock change at one store: sale, return, receipt, transfer, shrink, adjustment, salvage. Only some movements originate from a sale (docs/conceptual.md).'
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Composite relationships enforcing invariants
//
// DBML's inline `ref:` only expresses single-column foreign keys, so the
// relationships below are declared explicitly.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Both exist to make invariant I3 - a line's assortment belongs to the
// transaction's store - hold by construction rather than by trusting
// application code (docs/conceptual.md). Phase 3 needs the supporting unique
// constraint on sale_transaction (id, store_id), noted on that index above,
// for the first Ref to be valid SQL.
Ref: transaction_line.(sale_transaction_id, store_id) > sale_transaction.(id, store_id)
Ref: transaction_line.(store_id, product_id) > store_assortment.(store_id, product_id)

// Same discipline extended to inventory_movement: a movement's assortment
// belongs to the store it changed stock in.
Ref: inventory_movement.(store_id, product_id) > store_assortment.(store_id, product_id)

// transaction_line has no surrogate id (trimmed - see the phase-2 decisions
// note at the top of this file), so both references to "a specific line"
// are composite, pointing at its natural (sale_transaction_id, line_number)
// primary key directly.
Ref: transaction_line.(reverses_sale_transaction_id, reverses_line_number) > transaction_line.(sale_transaction_id, line_number)
Ref: inventory_movement.(line_sale_transaction_id, line_number) > transaction_line.(sale_transaction_id, line_number)
